WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:07.350
He came to Princeton as a young theorist to work near the man who had named the

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.770
hydrophobic interaction, and tried to compute its energetic cost straight from

3
00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.030
myoglobin's coordinates.

4
00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.310
The coordinates were too crude.

5
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:16.730
The project failed.

6
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:21.913
So he changed what he thought the mathematics needed: if the numbers were the

7
00:00:21.913 --> 00:00:24.233
problem, someone had to make better numbers.

8
00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:29.573
He learned X-ray crystallography, traded pure theory for a craft that pins atoms

9
00:00:29.573 --> 00:00:33.013
down, and only then could he turn the force into a quantity.

10
00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:39.437
"At last I was able to get down to energetic calculations on a protein, and came up

11
00:00:39.437 --> 00:00:43.057
with the idea of the hydrophobic moment," he said of the turn.

12
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:48.177
"This and related ideas gave me for the first time the feeling that I could make

13
00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:49.177
discoveries."

14
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.990
He spent the rest of his career watching hydrophobic burial go wrong: two beta

15
00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:58.630
sheets locked so tightly they squeezed out every water molecule, side chains

16
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.230
interlaced like a zipper's teeth.

17
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:06.063
He called it the steric zipper, and reduced a century of pathological clutter to one

18
00:01:06.063 --> 00:01:11.023
line: "We learned that the adhesive segments of the proteins that form these fibers

19
00:01:11.023 --> 00:01:12.563
are just short segments."
